  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions Using the Distributive Property">
    <p>Suppose three friends are going to the movies. They each need $$\text{\$9.25};$$ that is, $$9$$ dollars and $$1$$ quarter. How much money do they need all together? You can think about the dollars separately from the quarters.</p>
    <p>They need $$3$$ times $$\text{\$9},$$ so $$\text{\$27},$$ and $$3$$ times $$1$$ quarter, so $$75$$ cents. In total, they need $$\text{\$27.75}.$$</p>
    <p>If you think about doing the math in this way, you are using the Distributive Property.</p>
    <note type="Distributive Property">
      <p>If $$a,b,c$$ are real numbers, then</p>
      <equation>$$a{({b + c})} = ab + ac$$</equation>
    </note>
    <p>Back to our friends at the movies, we could show the math steps we take to find the total amount of money they need like this:</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{matrix} &amp; {3(9.25)} &amp; \\ {3(9} &amp; + &amp; {0.25)} \\ {3(9)} &amp; + &amp; {3(0.25)} \\ 27 &amp; + &amp; 0.75 \\ &amp; 27.75 &amp; \end{matrix}$$</equation>
    <p>In algebra, we use the Distributive Property to remove parentheses as we simplify expressions. For example, if we are asked to simplify the expression $$3(x + 4),$$ the order of operations says to work in the parentheses first. But we cannot add $$x$$ and $$4,$$ since they are not like terms. So we use the Distributive Property, as shown in Example 7.17.</p>
    <example number="7.17">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$3{({x + 4})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Demonstrates simplifying the expression 3(x+4) using the distributive property.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$3{({x + 4})}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell>$$3 \cdot x + 3 \cdot 4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$3x + 12$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.33">
      <p>Simplify: $$4{({x + 2})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.34">
      <p>Simplify: $$6{({x + 7})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Some students find it helpful to draw in arrows to remind them how to use the Distributive Property. Then the first step in Example 7.17 would look like this:</p>
    <example number="7.18">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$6{({5y + 1})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.35">
      <p>Simplify: $$9{({3y + 8})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.36">
      <p>Simplify: $$5{({5w + 9})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>The distributive property can be used to simplify expressions that look slightly different from $$a{({b + c})}.$$ Here are two other forms.</p>
    <note type="Distributive Property">
      <p>If $$a,b,c$$ are real numbers, then</p>
      <equation>$$a{({b + c})} = ab + ac$$</equation>
      <p>Other forms</p>
      <equation>$$a(b - c) = ab - ac$$</equation>
      <equation>$$(b + c)a = ba + ca$$</equation>
    </note>
    <example number="7.19">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$2{({x - 3})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.37">
      <p>Simplify: $$7{({x - 6})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.38">
      <p>Simplify: $$8{({x - 5})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Do you remember how to multiply a fraction by a whole number? We’ll need to do that in the next two examples.</p>
    <example number="7.20">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\frac{3}{4}{({n + 12})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.39">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{2}{5}{({p + 10})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.40">
      <p>Simplify: $$\frac{3}{7}{({u + 21})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="7.21">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$8\left( {\frac{3}{8}x + \frac{1}{4}} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.41">
      <p>Simplify: $$6\left( {\frac{5}{6}y + \frac{1}{2}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.42">
      <p>Simplify: $$12\left( {\frac{1}{3}n + \frac{3}{4}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Using the Distributive Property as shown in the next example will be very useful when we solve money applications later.</p>
    <example number="7.22">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$100{({0.3 + 0.25q})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.43">
      <p>Simplify: $$100{({0.7 + 0.15p})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.44">
      <p>Simplify: $$100{({0.04 + 0.35d})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>In the next example we’ll multiply by a variable. We’ll need to do this in a later chapter.</p>
    <example number="7.23">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$m(n - 4).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Notice that we wrote $$m \cdot 4\ \text{as}\ 4m.$$ We can do this because of the Commutative Property of Multiplication. When a term is the product of a number and a variable, we write the number first.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.45">
      <p>Simplify: $$r{({s - 2})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.46">
      <p>Simplify: $$y{({z - 8})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>The next example will use the ‘backwards’ form of the Distributive Property, $$(b + c)a = ba + ca.$$</p>
    <example number="7.24">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $${({x + 8})}p.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.47">
      <p>Simplify: $${({x + 2})}p.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.48">
      <p>Simplify: $${({y + 4})}q.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>When you distribute a negative number, you need to be extra careful to get the signs correct.</p>
    <example number="7.25">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$-2(4y + 1).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.49">
      <p>Simplify: $$-3(6m + 5).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.50">
      <p>Simplify: $$-6(8n + 11).$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="7.26">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$-11(4 - 3a).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>You could also write the result as $$33a - 44.$$ Do you know why?</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.51">
      <p>Simplify: $$-5(2 - 3a).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.52">
      <p>Simplify: $$-7(8 - 15y).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>In the next example, we will show how to use the Distributive Property to find the opposite of an expression. Remember, $$- a = -1 \cdot a.$$</p>
    <example number="7.27">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$- (y + 5).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiplying by −1 results in the opposite.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.53">
      <p>Simplify: $$- (z - 11).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.54">
      <p>Simplify: $$- (x - 4).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Sometimes we need to use the Distributive Property as part of the order of operations. Start by looking at the parentheses. If the expression inside the parentheses cannot be simplified, the next step would be multiply using the distributive property, which removes the parentheses. The next two examples will illustrate this.</p>
    <example number="7.28">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$8 - 2{({x + 3})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.55">
      <p>Simplify: $$9 - 3{({x + 2})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.56">
      <p>Simplify: $$7x - 5{({x + 4})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="7.29">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$4{({x - 8})} - {({x + 3})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.57">
      <p>Simplify: $$6{({x - 9})} - {({x + 12})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.58">
      <p>Simplify: $$8{({x - 1})} - {({x + 5})}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Evaluate Expressions Using the Distributive Property">
    <p>Some students need to be convinced that the Distributive Property always works.</p>
    <p>In the examples below, we will practice evaluating some of the expressions from previous examples; in part ⓐ , we will evaluate the form with parentheses, and in part ⓑ we will evaluate the form we got after distributing. If we evaluate both expressions correctly, this will show that they are indeed equal.</p>
    <example number="7.30">
      <problem>
        <p>When $$y = 10$$ evaluate:  ⓐ $$\ 6(5y + 1)$$ ⓑ $$\ 6 \cdot 5y + 6 \cdot 1.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$6(5y + 1)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify in the parentheses.</cell>
              <cell>$$6(51)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$306$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Add.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Notice, the answers are the same. When $$y = 10,$$</p>
        <equation>$$6{({5y + 1})} = 6 \cdot 5y + 6 \cdot 1.$$</equation>
        <p>Try it yourself for a different value of $$y.$$</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.59">
      <p>Evaluate when $${w = 3\text{:}}\$$ⓐ $$\ 5({5w + 9})\$$ⓑ $$\ 5 \cdot 5w + 5 \cdot 9.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.60">
      <p>Evaluate when $${y = 2\text{:}}\$$ⓐ $$\ 9({3y + 8})\$$ⓑ $$\ 9 \cdot 3y + 9 \cdot 8.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="7.31">
      <problem>
        <p>When $$y = 3,$$ evaluate ⓐ $$\ -2(4y + 1)$$ⓑ $$\ -2 \cdot 4y + {(-2)} \cdot 1.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\ -2(4y + 1)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify in the parentheses.</cell>
              <cell>$$-2(13)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$-26$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\ -2 \cdot 4y + {(-2)} \cdot 1$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$-24 - 2$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Subtract.</cell>
              <cell>$$-26$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The answers are the same. When $$y = 3,$$</cell>
              <cell>$$-2(4y + 1) = -8y - 2$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.61">
      <p>Evaluate when $${n = -2\text{:}}\$$ⓐ $$\ -6({8n + 11})$$ⓑ $$\ -6 \cdot 8n + {(-6)} \cdot 11.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.62">
      <p>Evaluate when $$m = -1\text{:}$$ⓐ $$\ \ -3(6m + 5)\$$ⓑ $$\ -3 \cdot 6m + (-3) \cdot 5.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="7.32">
      <problem>
        <p>When $$y = 35$$ evaluate ⓐ $$\ \text{−}(y + 5)$$ and ⓑ $$\ \text{−}\textit{y} - 5$$ to show that $$- (y + 5) = \text{−}\textit{y} - 5.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\ \text{−}(y + 5)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Add in the parentheses.</cell>
              <cell>$$- (40)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$-40$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\ - y - 5$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$-40$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The answers are the same when $$y = 35,$$ demonstrating that</cell>
              <cell>$$- (y + 5) = \text{−}y - 5$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.63">
      <p>Evaluate when $$x = 36\text{:}$$ⓐ $$\ \ - (x - 4)$$ⓑ $$\ - x + 4$$ to show that $$- (x - 4) = - x + 4.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="7.64">
      <p>Evaluate when $$z = 55\text{:}$$ⓐ $$\ \ - (z - 10)$$ⓑ $$\ - z + 10$$ to show that $$- (z - 10) = - z + 10.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Media">
      <list type="ul">
        <item>Model Distribution</item>
        <item>The Distributive Property</item>
      </list>
    </note>
  </subsection>
